package api

import (
	"net/http"
)

var _ = declareRoute(routeDecl{
	Method:      http.MethodGet,
	Pattern:     "/api/v1/issues/{key}/results",
	Auth:        authAny,
	Description: "The checks of the issue's Verification document as the block index holds each, in the order of its newest stored version, each with its attributes (when, surface, run, negative_control, ci_check, subject, program), the version its words last changed in and their digest, and every result recorded against it from any issue, newest first: {issue, verification_artifact_id, checks}. A check's attributes and words are read out of the text the block index recorded at that version, whose digest it carries, so a browser's edit that has not settled changes nothing here until its settlement indexes it, and a check the index does not hold yet is not listed. A check's text is its words with every link and dispatch:// reference dropped; a caller that sent Dispatch-Trees: 1 is also given text_tree, the check's whole content as the tree a reader is shown, links and references kept, and each result's observed_tree, the tree its observed text is shown as. A check's results come newest first by created_at, the time the recording transaction began, with id descending breaking a tie, and a client reading one result per check reads the first. Two results recorded in transactions that overlapped can come back in either order. Each check carries compared_with, the commits and branches its results are compared with, in the order they are asked about. For a before_merge check that is the head of each open pull request Dispatch holds for the check's issue and the final head of each merged one: the pull requests attributed to the check's issue, among them a pull request its external links name by its URL (https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/pull/<n>) in any repository unless its head is in a fork, each at the head its stored row holds now. Each is {kind: pull_request, commit, pull_request, merged, repo, learned_at: {at, kind}}, merged true for a merged pull request's final head, repo the owner/repo of a pull request in another repository than the one the check's issue resolves to, compared without case, which a pull request the issue links may be in (absent for a pull request of the issue's own repository, and for an attributed one wherever Dispatch cannot name the issue's repository), so a reader can tell it from a pull request of the same number in the issue's own repository, the kind of time being read_at when Dispatch last read that pull request from GitHub (checked_at) and updated_at when the pull request's own update time, as its newest webhook event carried it (event_at), is the later of the two. Where it holds none open, the check is also compared with the head of each branch that a result a CI job recorded on that issue names and that belongs to that issue (the head branch of one of its pull requests, or a branch whose name's first issue key that names an issue is that issue's), in name order, which it lists in branch_heads, {kind: branch_head, branch, commit}, and not in compared_with, so each field a dispatch command released before the branch heads reads keeps its meaning: commit is where GitHub answers the branch is on this read, and a head is left out where a pull request head names that commit or GitHub holds no such branch. For an after_merge check compared_with is {kind: branch, branch} for the branch that issue's pull request merged into and for the repository's default branch, the default branch alone where Dispatch holds no merge of the issue. For a default_branch check it is the repository's default branch alone. Where compared_with and branch_heads are both empty the check carries compared_nothing with its reason: no_pull_request_or_branch (the issue resolves to no repository, or a before_merge check's issue holds no pull request and no branch a CI job pushed), repository_unreadable (Dispatch's GitHub App cannot read the repository) or read_failed (a read Dispatch makes did not answer, the read of a pushed branch's head among them). Each check also carries pass_stands {answer, roles, reason, subjects, results}: yes with the roles whose passes stand, no, or unknown where the repository could not be read, for reason read_failed for a check CI proves with no subjects whose check runs GitHub could not be read (ci state unread), and for reason read_failed for a before_merge check of an issue with no open pull request whose merged pull requests Dispatch cannot order, one of them holding no time of its merge once Dispatch asked GitHub for it, which names no subject, so a pass at a merged head stands for nothing there. A pass stands when its commit is at one of the compared commits, the check's words have not moved since it was recorded, and no retraction stands against it. A before_merge check compared with heads asks that of each of its subjects: each open pull request's head, or where none is open, the newest merged pull request's final head, the one GitHub merged last (merged_at, read from GitHub once per read for a merged pull request of two or more whose time Dispatch does not hold), and each branch head above; a merged pull request's head beside an open one stays in compared_with and is no subject. It reads yes only where a pass stands at each, no where one holds only a fail or nothing, and unknown where none is no and one could not be read or is unknown; subjects lists each {basis, answer, reason, roles, results, ci}: reason fail (only a fail stands there), no_result (nothing does), pass_and_fail, or read_failed (the read that decides a check CI proves at that head did not answer, its check runs or the repository that says whether its CI job's result there is of that head's, which reads unknown whatever the other heads read); roles each role whose pass counts at that head, for yes alone; results the ids of the results that decide its state, the newest pass of each of those roles for yes, the newest fail for reason fail, the newest pass and the newest fail for pass_and_fail, and none for no_result and read_failed; ci, on a subject of a check CI proves, the read of its run at that head. The check's own roles then name a role only where each subject names it, in the order of the first subject. On a before_merge check, a pass and a fail that count at one commit, from any role and in either order, make that commit's state unknown, with reason pass_and_fail, and no pass stands there; on a check CI proves, each result its CI job recorded and the result derived from its check run count alike, and a head where the check's CI job recorded a result of its own for the check derives none from the run (case job_recorded), since the run's conclusion is the whole job's and a whole job's result decides no check. A later pass at that commit does not end that state; a retraction of the wrong one ends it, and so does a change of the check's words. A read with ?at= follows the same rule. An after_merge or default_branch check takes its newest result that counts at one of its branches, or that Dispatch could not place on one, with and without ?at=, and pass_stands.results names that result: yes only where it is a pass, with the roles of each pass at a branch newer than the newest fail there; no where it is a fail, or where no result decides; unknown where Dispatch could not place it. So a later fail cancels an earlier pass, and a deploy after the newest result changes no state here. Each result states two facts apart from each other. commit_stands {answer, basis, reason, repo} is where its commit stands against compared_with: at_basis with the basis it matched (for a check compared with branches, the branch that carries the commit; a basis in a repository the issue links a pull request in, rather than its own, carries that repository as repo, owner/name, whether it is the head of a pull request linked there, as in compared_with, or a branch of that repository, which a result whose commit is taken to be of it is compared with), other_commit, or unknown with the reason (repository_unreadable, read_failed); a before_merge result at no compared commit is unknown where the head of a branch a CI job pushed could not be read, since it may be that head. A before_merge result is at_basis only at a head of the repository its commit is taken to be of, the issue's own or a linked one, wherever the issue resolves to a repository, so a result of one repository never stands at the head of another's pull request at the same commit. An other_commit or unknown answer that a repository the issue links a pull request in decided, the one the result's commit is taken to be of, carries that repository as repo (owner/name), so a reader can tell it from an answer about the issue's own repository, which carries none. A result of results whose commit is a head of branch_heads answers other_commit there, and names that head in at_branch_head. check_words_moved is whether the check's words moved after the result was recorded: true where the result's check_sha256 is not the check's digest and its check_version is not the check's version, since a check whose stored text another renderer version spelled keeps its version under a new digest. The result's own check_version stands beside the check's version. A result a retraction stands against carries retracted {reason, actor, created_at} and counts as no evidence. With no ?at=, each result also answers currency (current or older) where either word applies, for dispatch clients older than pi-envoy 17.1.0; read commit_stands, check_words_moved and retracted instead. A check CI proves is one whose ci_check names a GitHub check run, with when before_merge, no run and a negative_control: its block's attributes, read on every read. A document write is held to a fourth rule too, that no result a role recorded stands against the block (400 INVALID_CI_CHECK), so a result a role recorded stands against such a check only until the settlement that takes its ci_check off; it carries not_counted, the reason it counts for nothing, and counts as no evidence. Dispatch reads that run at the head of each open pull request of the issue, each in the repository of that pull request's own row, else at the final head of its newest merged pull request, the one GitHub merged last, at most 8 heads at one time and every head under one deadline, and a head whose read did not end by then is unread there alone. The check carries ci {run, state, case, detail, line, commit, repo, result}, the read at the head of its open pull request of the highest number, else at that merged head: state pass, fail, no_result or unread; case the conclusion that decided it (success, failure, timed_out, action_required, startup_failure, stale, or another conclusion as GitHub wrote it), or neutral, skipped, cancelled, not_completed, absent (no run of that name at the head), no_head (Dispatch holds no open or merged pull request head of the issue), job_recorded (the run passed or failed at a head where the check's CI job recorded a result of its own for the check, a result that counts, of the head's repository, under the check's words now; that result decides the head), github_unreadable (state unread) or read_failed (state unread: the check's CI job recorded a result that counts at the head's commit and names no repository, and Dispatch's own read of the repository the issue resolves to, which says whether that result is of the head's, did not answer, so the run derives nothing there; or, naming no head, the issue has no open pull request and Dispatch cannot order its merged ones, one of them holding no time of its merge, so it reads no head); detail one sentence naming the case, with GitHub's own answer where GitHub could not be read; line the pull-request body line for the check; commit and repo the head read, and result the id of the result the read derived, for a pass or a fail. Its derived result at each head comes from that head's run on every read, for a pass or a fail alone and never for job_recorded or read_failed, and is stored nowhere: id ci-run:<check run id>, role ci, actor {kind: github_app, id: the slug of the App that made the run}, session \"\", verdict pass or fail, commit_sha the head read, created_at the run's completed_at, observed the conclusion, the run's output title and summary cut to 2,000 runes, and its URL, one a line, check_sha256 the check's own, and no event_seq. It is compared as a recorded result is, and stands at the head its run was read at. The results of such a check hold those derived results alone, as a dispatch command released before a CI job recorded results reads them, and recorded_results holds every result recorded against it, newest first: each one a run of the check's CI job recorded (role ci, run the check run's name, branch the branch whose push the job ran on), and each role's, which carries not_counted, as does a result whose run is another CI job's than the one the check's ci_check names. branch is set on a result a CI job recorded and on no other. Such a result also carries attestation, the in-toto Statement v1 of its run, built as the project's results route builds one: _type https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1; subject [{name: git+https://github.com/<the run's repository>, digest: {gitCommit: commit_sha}}]; predicateType https://github.com/cybermonklabs/legion/attestations/check-block-result/v1; predicate {project, check (the check's ref), procedure_digest (the result's check_sha256), test {runner \"\", selector \"\", job: the check's ci_check}, verdict, observed, job (the check run's whole name) and leg (\"\": this route reads no workflow file, and a name ending in parentheses proves no leg), run {repository, workflow_ref, ref, event_name, run_id, attempt, check_run_id, runner_environment}, recorder {session, principal}, recorded_at (the result's created_at)}. A result a role recorded and a derived result carry no attestation. ?at=<commit> replaces compared_with for every check of the issue by that one commit, {kind: named, commit}, and reads no branch head. The only read of GitHub it leaves is the check runs of a check CI proves, read at the issue's open pull request heads, or its newest merged one, as on every read, so a derived result stands at the named commit only where that commit is one of those heads. A value that is not 40 lowercase hex characters is 400 INVALID_QUERY. An issue with no Verification document answers no checks.",
	Handler:     (*server).listCheckResults,
	Read:        readEntry{Writes: readWritesNothing, WriteNote: "A read-only caller's read reads GitHub and stores nothing. A writer's read stores what it read from GitHub, as it always has: the pull request heads of a result's commit, and the refresh of a merged pull request whose time of merge an order of merges needs (pull_request_heads), with the branch commits (branch_commits) and the attribution events (events) that come with them. Like every reader of GitHub, the GitHub client may record GitHub's rate limit in github_rate_limits on a detached context, and the check blocks are read through the document service, whose cold read may queue a compaction (docsCompactionNote)."},
	Shape: routeShape{
		Status: http.StatusOK, Response: issueCheckResults{}, Params: issueResultsQuery{},
	},
})
